## Look hardest at
- ALL THREE explicit update maps carry the lease, including `handleRename`
  (:370-381) which also rewrites status and assignee. A field missing from one
  map is dropped with no error, leaving a synced clone `in_progress` with no
  expiry that nothing can steal there. Check each map individually; do not
  assume the diff covered them because it touched the file.
- The `checkFieldChanged` case: is the comparator nil-aware and built on
  `time.Time.Equal`? If it routes through the string helpers instead, every
  import reports an unconditional change — `updated_at` bump, event, dirty mark,
  export churn on every row. If the case is missing entirely, a lease-only
  renewal reads as unchanged and never propagates. Both failure directions
  matter.
- `TestClaimRoundTrip` must cover three paths: fresh store (exercises
  `insertIssue`), a store already holding the issue in its pre-claim state
  (exercises the update maps — where the drop happens), and a lease-only renewal
  on an `external_ref`-bearing issue (the only import path where the comparator
  is load-bearing, since its guard is `updated_at`, not the content hash). A
  test covering only the fresh store proves nothing about this bead.
- Would the round-trip test fail if the update-map changes were reverted? Say so
  explicitly.

